Note



Where this leaves us. Copy-paste is gone. One pipeline fits a model per group, whatever the group is. But fit_model() still hardcodes the response and predictor: it only ever fits body mass on flipper length. Block 5 removes that last limitation.

Embracing in practice
The fix:

summarise_by_species &lt;- function(data, var) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::summarise(mean = mean({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE), .by = species)
}

summarise_by_species(penguins_clean, body_mass_g)
# # A tibble: 3 x 2
#   species    mean
#   &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
# 1 Adelie    3701.
# 2 Gentoo    5076.
# 3 Chinstrap 3733.

Embrace the column every time it appears, and embrace the grouping column too if it is an argument:

summary_grouped &lt;- function(data, var, group) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::summarise(
      mean = mean({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE),
      sd   = sd({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE),
      .by  = {{ group }}
    )
}

summary_grouped(penguins_clean, body_mass_g, species)
summary_grouped(penguins_clean, body_mass_g, island)







Note



Under the hood (optional). { var } is shorthand for !!enquo(var). rlang::enquo() captures the argument as a quosure (the expression plus the environment it came from), and !! injects it back into the verb. You almost never need to write enquo()/!! by hand, but seeing the expansion demystifies the curly braces: they capture, then inject.

Strings and dynamic names
When a column name arrives as a string, embracing does not apply: strings are values, not expressions to capture. Use the .data pronoun in data-masking contexts:

summarise_by_string &lt;- function(data, var_name) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::summarise(mean = mean(.data[[var_name]], na.rm = TRUE), .by = species)
}

summarise_by_string(penguins_clean, "body_mass_g")

For output column names built from an argument, = is not enough: with = the name is used literally, so "{{ var }}_mean" = … makes a column called { var }_mean. The walrus operator := lets rlang fill in { var }, using glue-style name injection:

summarise_dynamic &lt;- function(data, var) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::summarise(
      "{{ var }}_mean" := mean({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE),
      "{{ var }}_sd"   := sd({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE),
      .by = species
    )
}

summarise_dynamic(penguins_clean, body_mass_g)
# # A tibble: 3 x 3
#   species   body_mass_g_mean body_mass_g_sd
#   &lt;chr&gt;                &lt;dbl&gt;          &lt;dbl&gt;
# 1 Adelie               3701.           459.
# 2 Gentoo               5076.           504.
# 3 Chinstrap            3733.           384.

Two things happen on the left of :=. "{{ var }}_mean" is glue-style injection, replacing { var } with the argument’s name; := is the assignment that lets rlang do that injection (with = the name is used literally). Both are needed.

Choosing the pattern
Activities 1 to 4 each drilled one pattern in isolation. In practice the hard part is deciding which pattern a situation calls for, because the answer depends on two independent questions: how does the column name arrive (bare name, string, or character vector), and what is the context (data-masking or tidy-selection)? This activity makes that decision the task.
Activity 5: choose the pattern


Activity 5: choose the pattern


For each scenario, name the correct forwarding pattern, then write the function. (a), (b) and (d) group by species.

var arrives as a bare name; compute its mean (data-masking).
var_name arrives as a string; compute its mean (data-masking).
cols arrives as a character vector; select those columns (tidy-selection).
var arrives as a bare name; compute its mean into an output column named &lt;var&gt;_mean (data-masking with a dynamic output name).





Solution


# (a) bare name, data-masking -&gt; {{ }}
summarise_mean &lt;- function(data, var) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::summarise(mean = mean({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE), .by = species)
}

# (b) string, data-masking -&gt; .data[[ ]]
summarise_mean_str &lt;- function(data, var_name) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::summarise(mean = mean(.data[[var_name]], na.rm = TRUE), .by = species)
}

# (c) character vector, tidy-selection -&gt; all_of()
select_cols &lt;- function(data, cols) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::select(species, dplyr::all_of(cols))
}

# (d) bare name, dynamic output name -&gt; {{ }} with :=
summarise_named &lt;- function(data, var) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::summarise("{{ var }}_mean" := mean({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE), .by = species)
}

Each scenario uses one of the four patterns summarised at the end of this block. The decision procedure is: first ask whether the name arrives bare, as a string, or as a vector; then, if it is a string or vector, ask whether the verb is data-masking (.data[[ ]]) or tidy-selection (all_of()). A bare name uses { } in either context.

Activity 6: predict the error


Activity 6: predict the error


Before running anything, predict what each call does: does it succeed, return the wrong answer, or error? Then explain why in terms of the masking model.

# (i) embracing a string
summarise_mean &lt;- function(data, var) {
  data |&gt; dplyr::summarise(mean = mean({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE), .by = species)
}
summarise_mean(penguins_clean, "body_mass_g")

# (ii) a string into a data-masking verb without .data
summarise_mean_str &lt;- function(data, var_name) {
  data |&gt; dplyr::summarise(mean = mean(var_name, na.rm = TRUE), .by = species)
}
summarise_mean_str(penguins_clean, "body_mass_g")





Solution


Returns the wrong answer (or a warning), not an error. { } captures the expression "body_mass_g", which is a string literal, not a column reference. mean() then receives a single character value, and mean() of a character returns NA with a warning. Embracing is for bare names; a string needs .data[[ ]].
Returns the wrong answer. var_name is not embraced, so dplyr first looks for a column called var_name, fails, then finds var_name in the function environment holding the string "body_mass_g". It computes the mean of that single string, again yielding NA. The string never reaches the data frame as a column reference because nothing told dplyr to treat it as one; that is the job of .data[[var_name]].

The general lesson: passing the wrong kind of object to a masked verb rarely errors loudly. It often produces an NA with at most a warning, which is more dangerous than an error. Match the pattern to how the name arrives.

