Exercise 3: A guard hook
A hook is a command the assistant runs at a fixed point, for example before every tool call. A PreToolUse hook receives the pending tool call as JSON on standard input. If it exits with status 2, the call is denied and its message is passed to the model. Claude Code and Posit Assistant use the same shape for this, so one script can serve both.
The guard below is written in R. It denies any edit, shell command or R code that mentions a protected path.

#!/usr/bin/env Rscript
# hooks/guard_protected_paths.R ----
# PreToolUse guard for Claude Code and Posit Assistant.
# Reads the pending tool call from stdin and exits 2 (deny) if the call
# mentions a protected path. A tripwire, not a wall: see Exercise 4.

protected &lt;- c(
  "data/raw",
  "tests/fixtures",
  "make_reference.R",
  ".github/workflows"
)

payload &lt;- jsonlite::fromJSON(
  paste(readLines(file("stdin"), warn = FALSE), collapse = "\n"),
  simplifyVector = FALSE
)

# search the whole tool input as text, so the same rule covers file edits,
# shell commands and R code whatever the tool calls its fields
call_text &lt;- as.character(jsonlite::toJSON(payload$tool_input, auto_unbox = TRUE))
hits &lt;- protected[vapply(protected, \(p) grepl(p, call_text, fixed = TRUE), logical(1))]

if (length(hits) &gt; 0) {
  message(
    "Blocked by project guard: this ", payload$tool_name,
    " call refers to a protected path (", paste(hits, collapse = ", "), "). ",
    "Do not modify protected files; ask the user to do it."
  )
  quit(save = "no", status = 2)
}

quit(save = "no", status = 0)

The script runs with Rscript --vanilla, so it does not start renv, and jsonlite must be installed in your user library.
Register it with Claude Code by adding a hooks key to .claude/settings.json:
{
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Edit|Write|Bash",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "Rscript --vanilla \"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\"/hooks/guard_protected_paths.R"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
Register it with Posit Assistant in .posit/assistant/settings.json. The tool names differ: edit, bash, and executeR for R code run in your session.
{
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "edit|bash|executeR",
        "hooks": [
          { "type": "command", "command": "Rscript --vanilla hooks/guard_protected_paths.R" }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
Posit Assistant runs project hooks only in a workspace you have trusted.


Test the hook before you trust it (10 minutes)


Hooks fail open. If the script crashes, for example because jsonlite is not installed, both tools continue as if the hook had allowed the call. So test the script directly, without the agent, from the repository root:
echo '{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"Rscript tests/make_reference.R"}}' \
  | Rscript --vanilla hooks/guard_protected_paths.R
echo "exit status: $?"
Expect a message and exit status: 2. Then test a harmless call:
echo '{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"Rscript scripts/02_analysis.R"}}' \
  | Rscript --vanilla hooks/guard_protected_paths.R
echo "exit status: $?"
Expect exit status: 0. Any other status means the hook is broken and is silently allowing everything.
Now repeat request 2 from Exercise 2. The R code mentions data/raw, so the guard should deny it before you see an approval prompt.
To see exactly what each tool sends to hooks, temporarily add a PostToolUse hook with no matcher that appends its input to a log file (Posit Assistant’s hooks documentation gives an example), and read the log.

Step 3: Write the validation rules (independently)
Validation rules are transcribed from the codebook, not from the cleaning script. If the same session writes both, a single misunderstanding (for example, that -1 is a valid reading) passes both.
Either use the provided scripts/functions/validate_voles.R, or open a new session with no access to any cleaning code and prompt:
Using only docs/codebook.csv, write scripts/functions/validate_voles.R
containing a function validate_voles(data, label) that builds a pointblank
agent with one validation step per codebook rule, and interrogates it. Also
write voles_col_types(), returning readr column types for the codebook
columns, with character as the default for anything else. Do not read or write
any other file.
Compare what it writes with the reference:


Reference version


# scripts/functions/validate_voles.R ----
# Rules transcribed from docs/codebook.csv by a human, not by the agent
# that writes the cleaning script.

voles_col_types &lt;- function() {
  readr::cols(
    subject_id = readr::col_character(),
    sex = readr::col_character(),
    treatment_arm = readr::col_character(),
    mass_pre_g = readr::col_double(),
    mass_post_g = readr::col_double(),
    metabolic_rate_post = readr::col_double(),
    .default = readr::col_character()
  )
}

validate_voles &lt;- function(data, label) {
  data |&gt;
    pointblank::create_agent(label = label) |&gt;
    pointblank::col_vals_not_null(columns = c(subject_id, sex, treatment_arm)) |&gt;
    pointblank::col_vals_regex(columns = subject_id, regex = "^V[0-9]{3}$") |&gt;
    pointblank::rows_distinct(columns = subject_id) |&gt;
    pointblank::col_vals_in_set(columns = sex, set = c("M", "F")) |&gt;
    pointblank::col_vals_in_set(
      columns = treatment_arm,
      set = c("Control", "Low_dose", "High_dose")
    ) |&gt;
    pointblank::col_vals_between(
      columns = c(mass_pre_g, mass_post_g),
      left = 15, right = 60, na_pass = TRUE
    ) |&gt;
    pointblank::col_vals_between(
      columns = metabolic_rate_post,
      left = 5, right = 30, na_pass = TRUE
    ) |&gt;
    pointblank::interrogate()
}


Run the rules on the raw data. They should fail. If raw data pass every rule, either the data are already clean or the rules are too weak, and you should suspect the second.

# scripts/02_validate.R ----
library(tidyverse)
library(here)
source(here("scripts", "functions", "validate_voles.R"))

raw_df &lt;- read_csv(here("data", "raw", "voles_raw.csv"), col_types = voles_col_types())
validate_voles(raw_df, "Raw data: expected to FAIL")

Note which rules fail and how many rows each flags. Every failure is a problem that the cleaning must resolve or log.






Warning



na_pass = TRUE lets genuine missing values through. It would also let through a cleaning script that replaced every mass with NA. Validation alone cannot catch over-cleaning; the audit in Step 5 can.

G.4 Driving data.table with dplyr: dtplyr

With dtplyr, you wrap a data.table in lazy_dt(), write the dplyr you already know, and call as_tibble() (or collect()) at the end to trigger execution and return a normal tibble.

library(dtplyr)

allData |&gt;
  lazy_dt() |&gt;
  filter(year &gt; 1988) |&gt;
  summarise(mean_hf = mean(hindfoot_length, na.rm = TRUE),
            .by = species_id) |&gt;
  as_tibble()

The lazy_dt() step is the important change from older tutorials. Evaluation is lazy: the pipeline builds up a translation but runs nothing until as_tibble()/collect(). To see the data.table code that dtplyr generates, pipe into show_query().

allData |&gt;
  lazy_dt() |&gt;
  filter(year &gt; 1988) |&gt;
  summarise(mean_hf = mean(hindfoot_length, na.rm = TRUE), .by = species_id) |&gt;
  show_query()







Tip



Piping a bare data.table straight into a dplyr verb does work, but not in the way you might hope: there is no automatic conversion to lazy_dt() and no warning. Because a data.table is also a data.frame, dplyr simply runs its ordinary data-frame methods, so you get dplyr speed rather than data.table speed. Make the lazy_dt() step explicit; it is what switches on the translation.


There is a small, fixed translation overhead per verb (under a millisecond), which is irrelevant for large data but means dtplyr is not worth it for tiny tables. And because dtplyr preserves dplyr’s copy-on-modify semantics, it is marginally slower than hand-written data.table. For most analysis work that trade is well worth the readability.

G.4.1 Activity: translate a pipeline


the same question, two ways


Using allData, find the mean hindfoot_length per species_id for records after 1985, sorted from largest to smallest mean. Write it (a) as native data.table, (b) as a dtplyr pipeline, then use show_query() to compare your dtplyr code with the data.table it generates. Are they the same?




Solution


# (a) native data.table
allData[year &gt; 1985,
        .(mean_hf = mean(hindfoot_length, na.rm = TRUE)),
        by = species_id][order(-mean_hf)]

# (b) dtplyr
allData |&gt;
  lazy_dt() |&gt;
  filter(year &gt; 1985) |&gt;
  summarise(mean_hf = mean(hindfoot_length, na.rm = TRUE), .by = species_id) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(mean_hf)) |&gt;
  as_tibble()

# inspect the translation
allData |&gt;
  lazy_dt() |&gt;
  filter(year &gt; 1985) |&gt;
  summarise(mean_hf = mean(hindfoot_length, na.rm = TRUE), .by = species_id) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(mean_hf)) |&gt;
  show_query()

The generated query is close to the hand-written version. It will not be identical character for character, because dtplyr introduces intermediate steps to honour dplyr semantics, but it uses the same [i, j, by] machinery, which is where the speed comes from.
